### 6. Joubert paper — M3 model role (detailed)

@c:\Users\vital\Desktop\JFDS-2022-Joubert-31-44.pdf

Read the paper and understand the M3 model role.

One point I would like you to think more carefully about is the role of M3. Without a clearly defined M3 layer, it is difficult to fully interpret the final asset-level or portfolio-level outcome. M1 and M2 can identify and filter candidate signals, but the realized portfolio result depends on how those signals are converted into allocation weights.

In particular, please clarify the meaning of a zero position in M3. In a long-only M1 setup, M1 = 0 means that no buy signal was generated. However, M3 = 0 can mean something different: M1 may have generated a buy candidate, but the final allocation was set to zero after applying M2 confidence or the position-sizing rule.

So I suggest distinguishing these cases clearly:

·       M1 = 0: no buy signal was generated.

·       M1 = 1 and M3 = 0: a buy candidate existed, but the model decided not to allocate capital to it.

·       M1 = 1 and M3 > 0: a buy candidate existed and received a positive portfolio weight.

This distinction is important because M3 is not just a technical add-on. It determines how M2's probability is translated into actual investment exposure, and therefore it directly affects return, Sharpe ratio, drawdown, and other portfolio-level metrics.

Also, I would be careful not to describe M3 as another classification model. In the standard meta-labeling setup, M1 is the primary signal model, M2 is the classification model that estimates the probability that M1's signal is profitable, and M3 is the position-sizing or allocation rule. In other words:

M2 output = probability that the M1 signal works
M3 output = how much to invest

M3 can still be simple. For example, you could use a threshold rule: if the M2 probability is above 0.60, take a full position; otherwise, take no position. In that case, M3 may look like a binary decision rule, but conceptually it is better to describe it as a threshold-based position-sizing rule rather than a separate classification model.

For the next step, I also recommend prototyping a simple M3 rule, even if M2 is not fully finalized yet. You could start with either a threshold rule or a simple probability-based sizing rule, then compare M1-only, M1+M2 filtering, and M1+M2+M3 sizing under the same evaluation framework.

